{
  "result": "The morning arrives without asking permission and I have learned to meet it halfway. My alarm is not really an alarm at all but a small bright rectangle of light that buzzes on the table beside my bed. I reach for it, squint, and sit up slowly because my knees have opinions that I have not yet agreed to respect. In the kitchen the kettle begins its low hiss before I even remember to flip the switch. I pour water over the grounds and watch the dark bloom rise, a small daily miracle that smells like earth and smoke and promise. While the coffee drips I open the window and listen to the street deciding what kind of day it wants to be. A bicycle passes, a dog barks once and thinks better of it, someone laughs on a balcony two floors up. I carry my mug to the small table where my notebook waits with its elastic band holding yesterday's unfinished thoughts. I do not write anything important, only a list of errands and half a sentence about wanting to learn how to fix things. Still it feels like a conversation, a quiet agreement between my future self and the person I am trying to become. Then I close the notebook, wash the mug, put on shoes that are slightly too tight, and step out into the bright ordinary air ready to see what happens next without any grand plan at all except to pay attention this time around.",
